$$z = \frac{-2 \sqrt {3}i}{3+ \sqrt {3}i}$$

To simplify this expression, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is $(3- \sqrt {3}i)$:

$$z = \frac{-2 \sqrt {3}i(3- \sqrt {3}i}{(3+ \sqrt {3}i)(3- \sqrt {3}i)} = \frac{-6\sqrt {3}i + 6\sqrt {3}i^2}{9 - 3i^2} = \frac{-6\sqrt {3}i - 6\sqrt {3}}{9 + 3} = \frac{-6\sqrt {3} - 6\sqrt {3}i}{12} = \frac{-3\sqrt {3} - 3\sqrt {3}i}{6} = \frac{-3\sqrt {3}}{6} - \frac{3\sqrt {3}}{6}i = -\frac{\sqrt {3}}{2} - \frac{\sqrt {3}}{2}i$$

Therefore, the point corresponding to $z$ in the complex plane is $\left(-\frac{\sqrt {3}}{2}, -\frac{\sqrt {3}}{2}\right)$.

The conjugate of $z$ is given by:

