In a Cartesian coordinate system, what is the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\)? Express the result in terms of its components.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \mathbf{i}F_x + \mathbf{j}F_y + \mathbf{k}F_z\), where \(F_x, F_y, F_z\) are scalar functions of \(x\) and \(y\), is given by:

\[
\mathbf{R} = \nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \mathbf{k} \\
F_x & F_y & F_z
\end{vmatrix}.
\]

Given that \(\mathbf{V}\) is only defined in terms of \(x\) and \(y\), we can write \(\mathbf{V} = F_x \mathbf{i} + F_y \mathbf{j} + 0 \mathbf{k}\). Thus, \(F_z = 0\). Substituting this into the curl formula:

\[
\mathbf{R} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & 0 \\
F_x & F_y & 0
\end{vmatrix}.
\]

Expanding this determinant along the third component (which is zero):

\[
\mathbf{R} = \left(-\frac{\partial}{\partialy}F_x\right)\mathbf{i} - \left(\frac{\partial}{\partialx}F_x\right)\mathbf{i} + 0 \mathbf{k} + \left(\frac{\partial}{\partialx}F_y\right)\mathbf{k} - \left(\frac{\partial}{\partialy}F_y\right)\mathbf{k}.
\]

Expanding the determinant along the third column:

\[
\mathbf{R} = \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y} F_x - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} F_y\right) \mathbf{k}.
\]

Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is:

\[
\mathbf{R} = \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y} F_x - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} F_y\right) \mathbf{k}.
\]

The components of the curl are:

\[
\mathbf{R} = \mathbf{i} \times 0 + \mathbf{j} \times 0 + \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y} F_x - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} F_y\right) \mathbf{k} = \boxed{\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y} F_x - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} F_y\right) \mathbf{k}}.
\]",fluency,"# Task Description
